The Last Rite ends very quickly. Like, the enemies barely have time to spawn and move toward the caster before it’s over. It should be a bit more dramatic and lengthened. 

Some intelligent undead have convictions – and some get strained/dislike their master. I actually think this is interesting/funny but I dislike that undead can actually LEAVE their master, as that makes no sense. (They are bound, after all, even if they hate what they do. ) 

Perhaps we can re-use the "ladder" of corpse degradation in a few more creative ways. For example, undead lieutenants leave a corpse behind the first few times they die, but every time you rez them after that, they degrade in quality. A fully rotten corpse finally just dissipates. But this at least gives lieutenants a chance to contribute and come back if they get smacked around in combat.

For some reason, the Gravehound uses a redoubt automaton skin/texture. Which is cute but confusing. (Probably from when we changed the redoubt creatures from animal texture to real automotons.) 

Casting shroud the dead seems to only work while the caster is extremely close. However, the purple “concentration” circle persists atop the undead/caster’s heads, making it seem like its active when it really isn’t.

Studying old formulae to convert into insight should also take time. It should not be an instant transfer type thing – but more like the usual research flow. Same with converting wood/stone into building materials – the craftsman should take a tad more time (sort of like the green bar built up when harvesting materials.) 

Research is still pretty fast in general, especially with multiple people at the bench. Let’s drag it out a bit more. 

